// The base clause grammar: where 'extends' may be written, and how often each
// clause may be.
//
// A type states two different things about what it is built on, and they are
// different assertions: 'is' names the abstractions it complies with, and
// 'extends' names the one concrete type it enriches with methods. So a type may
// write both, in either order -- struct-extends runs that -- and it states each
// of them once.
//
// A trait may write no 'extends' at all. An abstraction states requirements and
// holds no value, so there is nothing of it to enrich; what one abstraction
// asserts about another is conformance, which is 'is'.
//
// Parse stage: each of these is diagnosed as its clause is read, and a parse
// diagnostic stops the compile before name resolution, so nothing in this file is
// resolved and nothing else belongs in it. What may be enriched, and what an
// enrichment may declare, is a stage later, in struct-nameres-extends. An enum
// extending an enum is a third relationship that keeps the keyword --
// coneref/refenum.html, unimplemented -- so it is not refused here and there is
// nothing yet to assert about it.

trait Sensor {
  pub id i32;
}

struct Probe {
  pub id i32;
}

trait Logger extends Sensor {                //~ ErrorExtends:22 "there is nothing of it to enrich"
  pub id i32;
}

struct Twice extends Probe extends Probe {   //~ ErrorExtends:36 "enriches one concrete base"
}

struct Listed is Sensor is Sensor {          //~ ErrorExtends:28 "in one 'is' list"
  pub id i32;
}
